WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.835 --> 00:00:05.676
Presses, injection moulding machines and excavators work with hydraulics.

2
00:00:06.384 --> 00:00:10.587
Oil under high pressure, typically 100 to 350 bar,

3
00:00:10.691 --> 00:00:13.059
makes a large force in a small space.

4
00:00:14.075 --> 00:00:17.116
In confined oil, the pressure is the same everywhere.

5
00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:21.766
So it pushes on the whole piston, in the cylinder on top of the press.

6
00:00:22.773 --> 00:00:25.443
The force is the pressure times the piston area.

7
00:00:26.145 --> 00:00:32.414
A pressure of 1 bar is 0.1 newtons per square millimetre, so 200 bar is 20.

8
00:00:33.093 --> 00:00:37.644
A bore of 100 millimetres, the inside diameter of the cylinder,

9
00:00:37.818 --> 00:00:41.707
gives 7,854 square millimetres.

10
00:00:42.706 --> 00:00:50.845
Twenty times 7,854 is about 157 kilonewtons: the weight of 16 tonnes,

11
00:00:51.077 --> 00:00:52.168
from one cylinder.

12
00:00:53.465 --> 00:00:57.877
That oil comes from the hydraulic power unit: tank, motor and pump.

13
00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:02.718
A gear, vane or piston pump makes the flow: the litres per minute.

14
00:01:03.769 --> 00:01:07.624
The directional valve on the tank chooses where the oil goes.

15
00:01:08.368 --> 00:01:12.756
In one position it goes on top of the piston, and the ram moves down.

16
00:01:13.499 --> 00:01:17.168
In the other it goes underneath, and the ram comes back.

17
00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:22.925
The oil on the other side of the piston runs back, through the filter, to the tank.

18
00:01:23.986 --> 00:01:29.466
The cylinder speed, how fast the ram moves, is the flow divided by the piston area.

19
00:01:30.309 --> 00:01:34.442
A flow control valve throttles the flow and so sets the speed.

20
00:01:35.831 --> 00:01:37.920
A pump makes flow, not pressure.

21
00:01:38.653 --> 00:01:41.939
The pressure only rises as far as the load resists.

22
00:01:42.645 --> 00:01:46.024
While the ram comes down freely, the pressure stays low.

23
00:01:47.062 --> 00:01:49.280
Then the upper die meets the workpiece.

24
00:01:49.996 --> 00:01:53.943
The oil has nowhere left to go, and the pressure climbs fast.

25
00:01:54.997 --> 00:01:57.818
That is why there is always a pressure relief valve.

26
00:01:58.567 --> 00:02:00.935
Above a set value it opens to the tank.

27
00:02:01.687 --> 00:02:05.425
That caps the pressure, and protects pump, hoses and press.

28
00:02:06.429 --> 00:02:08.937
If the ram is held and the pump keeps running,

29
00:02:09.111 --> 00:02:11.630
all of the flow pours away over that valve.

30
00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:15.462
Every pressure drop across a valve turns into heat.

31
00:02:16.821 --> 00:02:19.201
Next to the pump stands an accumulator:

32
00:02:19.329 --> 00:02:22.440
a vessel with a bladder or piston and gas inside,

33
00:02:22.615 --> 00:02:23.741
usually nitrogen.

34
00:02:24.455 --> 00:02:28.542
It keeps oil under pressure, for fast movements or during a power cut.

35
00:02:29.637 --> 00:02:33.840
Switch the pump off and watch the gauge: the needle stays where it is.

36
00:02:34.607 --> 00:02:36.882
The accumulator stays pressurised.

37
00:02:37.897 --> 00:02:40.405
So making it safe for maintenance is more than

38
00:02:40.463 --> 00:02:42.866
switching off the motor and locking it out.

39
00:02:43.668 --> 00:02:46.594
First a safety block under the ram, or lower it.

40
00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.894
Only then bleed off the accumulator.

41
00:02:51.007 --> 00:02:55.128
Then check that the gauge really reads zero before you open anything.

42
00:02:55.851 --> 00:02:58.892
It is stored energy, just as in lockout-tagout.

43
00:03:00.307 --> 00:03:01.213
An example.

44
00:03:01.922 --> 00:03:06.125
This press cylinder has a bore of 125 millimetres,

45
00:03:06.322 --> 00:03:08.911
and the pump delivers 60 litres per minute.

46
00:03:09.963 --> 00:03:14.804
The piston area is 12,272 square millimetres.

47
00:03:15.519 --> 00:03:18.364
Sixty litres per minute is one litre per second.

48
00:03:19.102 --> 00:03:23.862
Divided by the area, that gives about 81 millimetres per second.

49
00:03:24.919 --> 00:03:30.260
Measured: only 60 millimetres per second, and the oil is at 70 degrees.

50
00:03:30.989 --> 00:03:34.182
So about a quarter of the flow never reaches the cylinder.

51
00:03:35.211 --> 00:03:37.893
Most likely a worn pump leaks internally,

52
00:03:38.079 --> 00:03:40.889
or a pressure relief valve opens too early.

53
00:03:41.575 --> 00:03:44.954
Both turn the lost flow into heat: hence the hot oil.

54
00:03:45.987 --> 00:03:51.026
Oil that keeps running above about 60 degrees ages fast, and seals harden.

55
00:03:51.757 --> 00:03:54.787
A common rule of thumb: for every 10 degrees hotter,

56
00:03:54.996 --> 00:03:57.341
the oil lasts roughly half as long.

57
00:03:58.666 --> 00:04:03.716
The main enemy is dirt: most failures come from dirt, water or air in the oil.

58
00:04:04.724 --> 00:04:10.042
How clean the oil is, you say with the ISO 4406 code.

59
00:04:10.814 --> 00:04:16.062
It counts the particles per millilitre larger than 4, 6 and 14 micrometres,

60
00:04:16.271 --> 00:04:19.975
as three code numbers, such as 18, 16, 13.

61
00:04:20.983 --> 00:04:25.720
Each step lower in the code is roughly half the particles: lower is cleaner.

62
00:04:26.765 --> 00:04:30.190
Good practice: filters chosen for the code you want to reach,

63
00:04:30.341 --> 00:04:33.627
sealed breathers on the tank, regular oil analysis,

64
00:04:33.812 --> 00:04:35.925
and never topping up from an open drum.

65
00:04:37.323 --> 00:04:41.433
Then a leak: this hose fitting sprays a jet of oil, as thin as a pin.

66
00:04:42.465 --> 00:04:44.532
Never feel for a leak with your hand.

67
00:04:45.289 --> 00:04:49.469
Hold a piece of cardboard in front of it: the cardboard shows the jet.

68
00:04:50.521 --> 00:04:55.048
A jet like that can force oil through the skin, even at modest pressure.

69
00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:57.702
That is an injection injury.

70
00:04:58.424 --> 00:05:02.081
It looks like a small prick, but it can cost a finger or a hand.

71
00:05:03.129 --> 00:05:07.576
Always a medical emergency: go straight to the emergency department,

72
00:05:07.726 --> 00:05:09.793
and say it is oil under high pressure.

73
00:05:10.493 --> 00:05:12.339
Do not wait until it hurts.
